 ## Subtopics
 
-### Extended Tandem Algorithm with Dumping
-This variant refers to the core sequential extension of the tandem algorithm that introduces the component dumping mechanism [S1]. It processes cross-sections sequentially, activating them one by one, simplifying the mesh, and dumping finished components [S1]. The loop runs from $k=1$ to $N-1$ (number of cross-sections), activating cross-section $k$, simplifying with $E_0$, and dumping [S1]. Finally, it activates all remaining components with infinity and simplifies again [S1].
+### Hierarchical Binary Partitioning
+A hierarchical scheme where the dataset is split recursively into sub-blocks to allow parallel extraction and merging. The dataset is split recursively in two parts perpendicularly to its longest direction as long as the sub-block size is greater than a given threshold. Isosurface extraction is performed on the leaf nodes using the Extended Tandem Algorithm, and surfaces are recursively merged to recompose the global surface. The threshold value is not specified in the sources [S1].
+
+### Parallel Tandem Algorithm with Time Lag
+A parallel processing method based on dataset splitting and the time lag extension. It ensures that parallel extraction generates a mesh with equivalent quality to the sequential approach. The key parameter is the isotropy parameter $\alpha$, recommended at 0.4 to prevent degenerated cases by ensuring the shape measure is always considered. Using $\alpha > 0.9$ causes the shape criterion to be ignored, leading to collapsed points far from the original surface [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: The parallel version significantly reduces extraction time compared to the sequential tandem algorithm [S1]. On a seismic dataset of size $1626 \times 7028 \times 2000$ (40 GB), extraction takes 5 minutes with 56 processors, compared to over three hours for the sequential tandem algorithm [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity**: By dumping completed components and processing blocks, the algorithm minimizes memory consumption [S1]. It avoids storing the entire isosurface in memory, which is critical for large datasets where the sequential algorithm fails due to memory limits (e.g., generating 1,250,000 triangles per layer in noisy datasets) [S1].
-- **Scalability**: The algorithm scales well with the number of processors, limited primarily by the communication overhead between workers and the manager [S1]. The hierarchical block splitting allows efficient load balancing [S1].
-- **Mesh Quality**: The parallel algorithm produces a mesh with equivalent quality (anisotropy variation) to the sequential approach [S1]. The use of time lag at block boundaries ensures that the mesh quality is not degraded by the parallel processing [S1].
-- **Strengths**: Handles very large datasets that exceed RAM; reduces memory footprint via dumping; maintains high mesh quality; enables interactive exploration via component indexing [S1].
-- **Limitations**: Requires a manager-worker infrastructure; complex task scheduling logic; performance depends on the distribution of surface components across blocks [S1].
+-   **Time Complexity**: Significantly faster than sequential Tandem. On a seismic dataset ($1626 \times 7028 \times 2000$, 40 GB), extraction took 5 minutes with 56 processors, compared to over 3 hours for the sequential algorithm [S2].
+-   **Memory Complexity**: Minimizes memory by extracting and simplifying in one pass per block and dumping completed components. Prevents the memory exhaustion seen in brute-force approaches or the sequential Tandem on very large datasets [S1, S2].
+-   **Scalability**: Scales well with the number of processors and block splitting. The hierarchical splitting allows efficient load balancing [S2].
+-   **Mesh Quality**: Produces meshes with equivalent anisotropy to the sequential Tandem when $\alpha=0.4$ and time lag is correctly applied. Anisotropy ranges from 0 (equilateral) to 1 (flat) [S2].
+-   **Strengths**: Handles very large geophysics datasets; reduces triangle count drastically compared to classical marching cubes; enables interactive exploration via component-based file organization [S1, S2].
+-   **Limitations**: Requires a master-worker infrastructure; complex task scheduling logic; dependent on efficient I/O for dumping [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-The Extended Tandem Algorithm was introduced by Guilhem Dupuy and Bruno Jobard in 2009 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of the original Tandem Algorithm [3675-tandem-algorithm.md], which, while effective for large datasets, suffered from limited scalability on very large, noisy datasets due to high memory consumption during queue updates and simplification [S1]. The extension was motivated by the need to process geophysical datasets (such as seismic cubes) that are often large and contain numerous small, disconnected components [S1]. The method builds on the tandem algorithm's simplification strategy and incorporates component dumping and parallel block processing to enable the extraction of isosurfaces from datasets exceeding available memory [S1].
+The Tandem Algorithm was introduced by Attali et al. in 2005 to reduce memory consumption by alternating extraction and simplification [S1]. The Extended Tandem Algorithm was developed to address the limited scalability of the Tandem Algorithm on very large, noisy datasets (e.g., $1626 \times 7028 \times 2000$), where queue updates became too memory-intensive [S1, S2]. It was proposed for use in geophysics, specifically for seismic cubes used in hydrocarbon exploration, where datasets are large and contain numerous small disconnected components [S1, S2]. The work was supported by Total company [S1].
 
